        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Ah, this might be a problem because integrations are designed to only add whole line highlighting and not text. I don't think this design it will change, unfortunately.\r\n\r\nThe best one can do with 'mini.map' is to have line highlighted if there is *any* mark there. Not sure how this can be useful, but here is how to do it:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal mark_integration = function()\r\n  local source_buf_id = MiniMap.current.buf_data.source\r\n  local res = {}\r\n  for _, mark_data in ipairs(vim.fn.getmarklist(source_buf_id)) do\r\n    table.insert(res, { line = mark_data.pos[2], hl_group = 'Special' } )\r\n  end\r\n  return res\r\nend\r\nrequire('mini.map').setup({ integrations = { mark_integration } })\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis also has the issue that integrations are designed to update whenever there *buffer content* change. As placing mark manually (like with `mk`) does not modify content, map's integration display will not get updated. This is mostly for performance reasons to not have map update integrations on every cursor move. There are probably ways to force integration update (with `MiniMap.refresh({}, { lines = false, scrollbar = false })`) whenever mark is placed, but they seem to be hacky as there is no dedicated event for that (unlike `DiagnosticChanged` for diagnostic, etc.).\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nSo I am afraid, showing mark id as text in integrations is not (and most probably) will not be possible. The best I can suggest is `:Pick marks` mapped to some easy to type keys.",
